WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.060
As the climate warms, species are on the move.

2
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.640
Across many studies of land species, ranges have shifted toward the poles and up

3
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:17.020
mountains, by a median of about 17 kilometres and 11 metres every decade.

4
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.920
Species vary a lot around those numbers.

5
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.260
In the sea, species have moved poleward about 6 times faster.

6
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:29.560
That reshuffling is already changing which species live together, and it matters for

7
00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.300
ecosystems and for people.

8
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:38.640
How do we know? In 2011, I-Ching Chen, Chris Thomas and colleagues pooled published

9
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.640
range shifts into one meta-analysis.

10
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.180
Shifts were biggest where the climate had warmed the most.

11
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.320
Poleward shifts roughly kept pace with warming.

12
00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:49.820
Uphill shifts mostly fell short.

13
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.360
The lesson's worked problem shows how to measure that shortfall on a mountain.

14
00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:56.573
Why does it matter?

15
00:00:57.673 --> 00:01:01.293
Species do not move together, so communities come apart and reassemble.

16
00:01:02.353 --> 00:01:04.493
Hosts and pathogens can meet in new places.

17
00:01:05.473 --> 00:01:10.313
A 2017 review traced the effects of these moves to food production, the spread of

18
00:01:10.313 --> 00:01:13.313
disease, and how much carbon land and sea store.

19
00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:16.047
Climate is not acting alone.

20
00:01:16.987 --> 00:01:22.207
Clearing land for farms and towns is the largest single driver of biodiversity loss

21
00:01:22.207 --> 00:01:29.407
on land. One model estimate put the average loss of local species richness at 13.6%.

22
00:01:30.247 --> 00:01:34.187
In warmer regions, forest loss also tended to speed up uphill shifts.

23
00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:38.827
And species carried by people are among the most common threats linked to

24
00:01:38.827 --> 00:01:39.827
extinctions.

25
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.190
Warming also shifts timing.

26
00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:47.670
Many spring events now come earlier, but not by the same amount for every species.

27
00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:52.490
When a bird's breeding moves less than its caterpillar food, chicks can miss the

28
00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:58.690
peak. In Dutch pied flycatchers, populations fell by about 90% % where caterpillars

29
00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:02.710
peaked early. Fewer chicks do not always mean fewer birds, though.

30
00:02:03.683 --> 00:02:09.083
In the sea, dissolved carbon dioxide lowers pH and the carbonate that shells and

31
00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:10.303
skeletons are built from.

32
00:02:11.083 --> 00:02:15.763
On average, calcification, growth and survival fall, but groups differ widely.

33
00:02:16.723 --> 00:02:20.463
On land, nitrogen from polluted air can cut plant diversity.

34
00:02:21.463 --> 00:02:26.143
On Minnesota prairie, a modest extra dose lowered species number by 17%.

35
00:02:26.143 --> 00:02:27.143
percent.

36
00:02:27.517 --> 00:02:30.677
So when a range moves uphill, that shows a change happened.

37
00:02:31.657 --> 00:02:32.817
Scientists call that detection.

38
00:02:33.697 --> 00:02:34.957
It does not show the cause.

39
00:02:36.177 --> 00:02:40.257
Attribution links the change to a cause, and tests the alternatives, such as forest

40
00:02:40.257 --> 00:02:43.617
loss. Test the alternatives before you name the cause.
